Black Box testing - put a set of inputs into a code and you get a set of outputs. You don't know what's happened in between, but the testing is 'for this set of inputs, I should see this set of outputs'. Don't care what's going on in between, just care about results.
White box testing - Care about what happens. Check all the changes, check everything that happens as it goes through. White box testing, check all bits in the system. Like me testing every individual SAS step back in the old days.

Unit testing is very often white box testing, so checking everything. Check things work in all circumstnaces etc
- Normal values
- Extreme values
- Broken values, various types of fault and make sure it correctly rejects when it hits an exception.

Requirements document being generated and then giving it to the end user community to run acceptance tests. World will have moved on. Requirements document will have moved on compared to current business needs. May be things they realise that they need when compared to the system. Can have a system pass system testing, but then in the hands of users, well this is no use it doesn't do this. Could be a new requirement, or that the world has changed and consequently things are different.


