Chaos monkey! 
Netflix use AWS. When you start watching a film you're allocated a stream off a server that's sending your local device hte movie. What they have is this background process that goes around and kills the services? PIcks up failed service, 
Recovery is faster than buffering. Randomly do it to the services that are live running. The chaos monkey is a process that goes arund and kills other processes at random, to make sure that the recovery environment works correctly. Makes sure that what you think will happen actually happens.

e.on have 3 data centres, 2 in germany and 1 in the UK. Configured so that one is primary, then secondary, then secondary secondary. In the event that the primary fails and they go to secondary, that secondary already has a secondary that's ready to go.
Three tiems a year they go and deliberately fail the primary data centre, not gracefullly, they destroy the primary data centre so that the secondary data centre takes over, and secondary secondary becomes a true secondary, and then the primary data centre is then reconfigured to be the secondary secondary. So the 3 data centre actually rotate throughout the year. That takes quality testing to the logical extreme. They know their systems work under failure conditions because they fail them 3 times a year.
Need to test failures. Test bad data, to make sure the system gracefully handles that.

